
{"id":"24769509-99fc-486a-9054-6f96e8daeb77","object":"chat.completion","created":1784832873,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"import json\\nfrom collections import defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if 'payload' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing payload\\\")\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if 'nodes' not in payload or 'required_symbols' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing nodes or required_symbols\\\")\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    symbol_nodes = defaultdict(set)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(nid)\\n        symbol_nodes[node['symbol']].add(nid)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        if node.get('parent') is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Multiple roots\\\")\\n            root_id = nid\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root\\\")\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if not symbol_nodes.get(sym):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol {sym} not found\\\")\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    req_symbol_retained = {sym: set(symbol_nodes[sym]) for sym in required_symbols}\\n    removal_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(rid in retained for rid in req_symbol_retained[sym]):\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids,\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements a grammar-guided node reduction algorithm. The task is essentially a reduction of a parse tree (nodes with parent, depth, symbol, removable, utility). The goal is to remove nodes (tentatively) while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we don't have a grammar. The fixture shows nodes with parent IDs, symbol, removable flag, utility. The case has `required_symbols`. The algorithm should traverse nodes depth-first? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we need to process nodes in order of decreasing depth, and within same depth use node ID as tie-breaker? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" But also \"registered_candidate_order\" suggests ordering candidates in a specific way. And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we iterate over nodes (sorted by depth descending, then by node ID ascending maybe? Since tie break at equal depth, we need a stable order; typically ascending ID is stable). For each node that is removable, we check if removing it (and potentially its subtree?) is valid: the node's descendants would also be removed? The problem says \"reduction\" which typically means removing nodes that are not required and not ancestors of required symbols. But we also have \"grammar-valid\" but no grammar rules. Possibly the grammar is implicit: there is a symbol hierarchy? The node symbols are like \"root\", \"s1\", \"s2\", \"s0\". But we don't have rules. Possibly the algorithm is simply to remove as many removable nodes as possible while ensuring that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol in the tree after removals. And also we cannot remove a node that has retained children (because that would orphan them? Actually if we remove a node, its children should be removed too? The root cannot be removed. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if we remove a node, its children are also removed (since they are only reachable through it). So we must ensure that before removing a node, none of its descendants are required or have required children? Wait: \"or a node with retained children.\" Means if any child is retained (not removed), we cannot remove the parent. So the removal is bottom-up: we only remove leaf nodes? But deeper nodes are considered first, so we remove from bottom up. And we only remove a node if all its descendants are already removed? Actually \"with retained children\" - if a child is retained, parent can't be removed. So the removal order is such that children are removed before parents. So we need to simulate: we start with all nodes. We iterate nodes in depth descending order. For each node, if it is removable and not required (and not root), and if removing it does not cause any required symbol to become missing (considering that its subtree includes its descendants, but if any descendant is required, we cannot remove). Also must check that the node's symbol is not in required_symbols? \"Never remove the root, a required symbol\" - so if node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it. And also if any descendant has a required symbol? Actually the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" means after removal, there must still be at least one node (anywhere) for each required symbol. So if a required symbol appears only in the subtree of this node, removal would lose that symbol, so we cannot remove. So we need to check that for every required symbol, there exists at least one node not in this node's subtree (including the node itself) that has that symbol. That is necessary.\n\nAlso the node must not have any retained children. Since we process bottom-up, children will have been considered earlier. But we need to track which nodes are retained or removed. So we need a data structure: initially all nodes are considered \"retained\" (not removed). We will mark nodes as removed during processing. When processing a node, we need to know if any of its children are still retained. If so, we cannot remove it. So we must check: for every child (direct child, by parent field) that is still retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the node. Since we process children first, they should already be removed if possible. So it's consistent.\n\nAdditionally, the algorithm might also consider \"replacement candidates\" but the description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". The fixture doesn't show any replacement info. Maybe replacement is just removal? Or maybe we need to consider moving children to parent? Not sure.\n\nThe output: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to return two lists: retained_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that are retained after reduction), and removal_order (list of IDs in the order they were removed). Also \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So the solve function should return a dict with keys like retained_ids and removal_order? The instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" Probably the return value should be something like {\"retained_ids\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}.\n\nBut the task says \"Implement grammar_guided_node_reduction through `solve(case)`\" and \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to decide the exact field names. Looking at the fixture payload, there's no specification of output shape. Typically in such problems, the output is a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". But we must be careful: the instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It does not specify the keys. I'll assume keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". Also \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if the case is invalid (e.g., missing required fields, cycles, etc.), raise ValueError.\n\nNow, the algorithm details:\n\n1. Validate input: case must have \"payload\" with \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list. Each node must have \"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\". Check that root exists? Probably.\n\n2. Build a map from node id to node dict. Also map from id to children list. Ensure no cycles and parent-child consistent.\n\n3. Check that root has parent null and depth 0, and symbol \"root\"? Not necessarily but root must be present.\n\n4. Determine order: sort all nodes (except root?) by depth descending, then by node id ascending (stable tie-break). Actually we should process all removable nodes, but including root? root is not removable. So sort nodes that are removable? The algorithm will consider each node in that order.\n\n5. Initialize retained set with all node ids. Also removal_order list.\n\n6. For each node in sorted order (by depth desc, id asc):\n   - If node is not removable, skip.\n   - If node's symbol is in required_symbols, skip (cannot remove required symbol).\n   - If node has any child that is currently retained (i.e., in retained set), skip.\n   - Check that after removing this node (and its entire subtree - but note that its children may already be removed, so we only need to remove this node from retained set; its descendants are already removed if they were removable? Actually if a child is still retained, we skip. If child is not retained because it was removed earlier, that's fine. But what about non-removable children? If a child is non-removable, it will be retained. Then parent cannot be removed because child is retained. So that's correct.\n   - Check that for every required symbol, there exists at least one node in retained set (excluding this node and its retained descendants? Actually we are about to remove this node. So after removal, the retained set will lose this node. We need to ensure that each required symbol still has at least one node somewhere else. So we can check before removal: for each required symbol, count occurrences in current retained set. If removing this node would reduce count to zero for some required symbol, then skip. (Note: if the node itself has that symbol, and there are no other nodes with that symbol, removal would lose it.)\n   - If all checks pass, remove this node: remove from retained set, add its id to removal_order.\n\n7. After processing all nodes, sort retained_ids (ascending, presumably by id string? Or numeric? IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"root\". They might be comparable lexicographically. We'll just sort the list of ids alphabetically? But the fixture shows IDs like \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. Sorting alphabetically would put \"n1\" before \"n10\"? But IDs seem to be just string identifiers. We'll use natural sorting? Simpler: sort as strings; the problem likely expects stable order. Since IDs are unique and not numeric, sorting string is fine.\n\n8. Return dict {\"retained_ids\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nBut is that the full algorithm? The description also mentions \"grammar-valid\" but no grammar. Possibly the grammar is implicitly defined by the parent-child relationships? Or maybe we need to consider that certain symbols can only appear under certain parents? But no info. The task says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we only have deletion. I'll assume the algorithm is as above.\n\nAlso \"registered_candidate_order\" suggests that the order in which we consider nodes matters. We are using depth descending and then node id ascending.\n\nAlso \"stable_ties\" – we are stable.\n\nNow implement solve(case). We'll parse payload, build structures, validate.\n\nPotential edge cases: multiple roots? The case has one root with parent null. We'll assume that.\n\nWe'll raise ValueError if required symbol not found initially? That would be infeasible because removal would lose it. But we could still process: root always present. Actually if a required symbol is missing from the entire tree, the case is invalid? Probably yes, because we cannot preserve it. But the fixture shows root and s1 are always present. We'll raise ValueError if any required symbol is not present in the initial set.\n\nAlso, if after removal, required symbols still present.\n\nImplementation details: use a dictionary for retained set, and a set for node ids. Use set for fast membership.\n\nWe'll need to build children list: for each node, we need its direct children (by parent field). So create dict children where key parent id, value list of child ids.\n\nThen during processing, to check if node has retained children, look up in children dict and check if any child id is in retained set.\n\nTo check required symbol coverage: we can precompute a mapping from symbol to list of node ids that have that symbol. Then for each candidate removal, we can check if the node's symbol is the only one for that symbol. So we need to maintain current counts. Updates: when we remove a node, decrement count for its symbol. We need for each required symbol, ensure count > 0 after removal.\n\nSimplify: before removal, check if for each required symbol, there exists some node (other than this node) with that symbol. So we can compute a set of node ids that have each required symbol, and then check if the set size > 1 or if the node's id is not in the set? But after removal, if there are multiple nodes with same symbol, it's fine. So we can precompute for each required symbol a set of node ids that have that symbol. Then during removal, if the node's symbol is not required, no problem. If it is required, we need to check if there is another node with that symbol in retained set. Since we haven't removed the node yet, we can check if the set for that symbol has size > 1 (considering only retained nodes). But other nodes might have been removed already, so we need to maintain the current set of retained nodes for each required symbol. So we can maintain a dict symbol_to_retained_ids (set). Initially all nodes present. When we remove a node, we remove it from the set for its symbol. Then before removal, we check if the set for its symbol would become empty after removal.\n\nThus we need to keep track of current retained set of nodes for each symbol. So we initialize symbol_to_retained = defaultdict(set) with node ids for each node's symbol. Then while processing, we check if node's symbol is in required_symbols: if len(symbol_to_retained[sym]) == 1 and node.id in that set, then removing would lose it; skip. Also if node's symbol is not required, no issue.\n\nThis is simpler.\n\nNow, also we need to consider that we cannot remove a node that is a required symbol. So before anything, we skip nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nNow implement solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if 'payload' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing payload\")\n    payload = case['payload']\n    if 'nodes' not in payload or 'required_symbols' not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing nodes or required_symbols\")\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = defaultdict(list)\n    symbol_to_ids = defaultdict(set)\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map[parent].append(nid)\n        # Check required fields\n        if 'symbol' not in node or 'removable' not in node:\n            raise ValueError(\"Missing fields in node\")\n        symbol_to_ids[node['symbol']].add(nid)\n    # Check root exists\n    root_id = None\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            if root_id is not None:\n                raise ValueError(\"Multiple roots\")\n            root_id = nid\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root\")\n    # Check required symbols all present\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if len(symbol_to_ids.get(sym, set())) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not found\")\n    # Initialize retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_retained = {sym: symbol_to_ids[sym].copy() for sym in required_symbols}\n    # Also include other symbols? not needed for check.\n    removal_order = []\n    # Sort nodes: all nodes except maybe root? We'll include root but it's not removable.\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check children: any child still retained?\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child in children_map.get(nid, []):\n            if child in retained:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols: if removing this node (and its subtree) would lose any required symbol\n        # Since we only remove this node (children already removed if possible), we only need to check the node's own symbol if it is required? But node's symbol is not required because we skipped that. However, what about descendants that might have required symbols? If a descendant has a required symbol, that descendant would have been retained because we cannot remove a node with retained children? Actually if a descendant has a required symbol, that descendant is not removable because its symbol is required. So it will be retained. Then its parent cannot be removed because the parent has a retained child (the descendant). So by the time we consider this node, any required symbol descendant would have forced the child to be retained, which would prevent removal. So we don't need to check further? But what if a descendant with required symbol is removable? It can't be because required symbol nodes are not removable. So that's fine. But what about required symbols that appear in descendants that are not directly children? Actually if a descendant has a required symbol, that descendant will be retained and thus its ancestors cannot be removed because they have retained children. So it's safe. However, there is a scenario: a node could have a required symbol in its subtree but the required symbol node is already removed? No, required symbols cannot be removed. So it remains. So the only required symbol that could be lost upon removal of this node is if the node itself has a required symbol (already skipped) or if the required symbol is in a descendant that is not retained? But retained means not removed. Since we remove only nodes that are removable and not required, the required symbol nodes are always retained. So they will block removal via child retention. So the check for required symbols after removal is not necessary if we enforce child retention? Let's double-check: Suppose we have root -> n1 (removable) -> n2 (required symbol, removable? No, required symbol nodes are not removable because we skip them). So n2 is retained. Then when checking n1, it has child n2 retained -> cannot remove n1. So fine. What about root -> n1 (removable) -> n2 (not required, removable) -> n3 (required symbol). n3 is retained, so n2 has child n3 retained -> cannot remove n2. Then n1 has child n2 retained (because n2 not removed) -> cannot remove n1. So all ancestors are blocked. So the only way a required symbol could be lost is if we remove a node that is not an ancestor of that required symbol? Actually if the required symbol is elsewhere, it's unaffected. So the check for required symbols after removal is redundant given child retention and that we never remove required symbol nodes. However, consider a case where the required symbol node has been removed? Not allowed. So we can omit the explicit required symbol count check. But to be safe and match description \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" we could still check. But it's not necessary. I'll include it for thoroughness.\n\n        # Check required symbols after removal: for each req sym, ensure there is still at least one node in retained set that has that symbol (including possibly the current node if it had it, but we already skipped if symbol required). So we need to consider that the node might have a required symbol in its subtree that is being removed implicitly? No, because children are not removed with the node? Actually if we remove a node, we are only removing the node itself, not its children. Wait, the problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Does removal of a node remove its subtree? Usually in tree reduction, removing a node also removes its descendants because they become inaccessible. But the data structure is a flat list of nodes with parent pointers. If we remove a node, its children remain in the list but their parent is still the removed node. That would be invalid. Typically in such reduction, when you remove a node, you either replace it with its children or delete the entire subtree. The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". It mentions both deletion and replacement. However, the fixture doesn't specify replacement. Given the instruction \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The condition \"node with retained children\" implies that if a node has children that are going to be retained, you cannot remove the node. So if you remove a node, its children must also be removed (i.e., not retained). So the removal of a node implies removal of its entire subtree. That matches the condition: you cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained, meaning the subtree must be removed entirely. So when we remove a node, we are effectively removing that node and all its descendants that are still retained? Actually we process bottom-up: children are removed first if possible. So when we get to a node, its children may have been already removed. If they are still retained, we cannot remove this node. So if we remove a node, it means all its children have already been removed (or never existed). So we are only removing the node itself, not its children (they are already gone). So the subtree is removed piece by piece. So the only node being removed at this step is the current node. So the children are already not in retained set. So we don't need to recursively remove descendants. So the check for required symbols: we only need to ensure that the current node's removal doesn't cause a required symbol to disappear. But the current node's symbol is not required (we skip). So no required symbol is lost. However, what if a required symbol was present in a descendant that has already been removed? That would be an error because required symbol nodes are never removed. So they are still retained. So they are not descendants of this node because they are still in the tree (with parent pointers). Actually if a required symbol node is a descendant of this node, it would have been processed earlier (deeper) and could not be removed because its symbol is required. So it is still retained, and its parent (which is this node or an intermediate) would have a retained child, preventing removal. So this node would fail the child retention check. So we never encounter a situation where removing a node would cause loss of a required symbol that is its descendant. So the check is redundant. But we'll still include a general check: for each required symbol, count occurrences in current retained set. If the node's symbol is in required_symbols (but we already skip), else fine. Actually we can skip.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll implement the check: before removal, for each required symbol, we ensure that there exists at least one node with that symbol in retained set that is not this node? Since this node's symbol is not required, we don't need to exclude. So we can just check that each required symbol has at least one occurrence in retained set (it already does, and removal of this node doesn't change that because the node doesn't have that symbol). So it's always true. So we can skip. But to be safe, I'll keep the code structure with a check that passes.\n\nThus the main algorithm is simple.\n\nNow implement solve with these steps.\n\nWe'll return dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". Convert to JSON serializable (list of strings). Ensure retained_ids sorted.\n\nNow test with given fixtures? The fixture has three cases. We can simulate manually to verify.\n\nCase 0: nodes: root (depth0), n1 (s1,d1,removable false), n2 (s2,d1,removable true), n3 (s0,d1,removable false), n4 (s1,d2,parent n2, removable true), n5 (s2,d2,parent n3, removable false), n6 (s0,d2,parent n1, removable true). required_symbols: root, s1.\n\nProcess depth descending: depth2: n4 (removable true, symbol s1 required? s1 is required, so skip), n5 (removable false skip), n6 (removable true, symbol s0 not required). Check children: none (no children). Check required symbols: all present (root, s1 have nodes). So remove n6. removal_order = ['n6']. retained set loses n6.\n\ndepth1: nodes: n1 (removable false skip), n2 (removable true, symbol s2 not required), n3 (removable false skip). Process n2: children: n4 (still retained? n4 is not removable because symbol s1 required, so retained). So has retained child -> skip n2. So no more removals. Retained ids: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sort: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? But root is also retained. So retained_ids = ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? Actually sorting strings: 'n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root'? 'root' comes before 'n1'? Lexicographically, 'n' is after 'r'? 'n' (110) vs 'r' (114) so 'root' < 'n1'? Actually 'r' (114) > 'n' (110), so 'n1' < 'root'. So order: 'n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root'. That seems plausible.\n\nCase 1: similar but different utilities. Root, n1 (s1,nonremovable), n2 (s2,removable), n3 (s0,nonremovable), n4 (s1,removable), n5 (s2,nonremovable), n6 (s0,removable). Depth2: n4 (s1 required skip), n5 (nonremovable), n6 (s0,removable, no children, check required symbols: s1 still present via n1 and n4, root present, so remove n6. removal_order: ['n6']. Depth1: n2 (s2,removable) has child n4 (s1 required, retained) -> skip. n1 nonremovable, n3 nonremovable. So retained: all except n6. Same as case0.\n\nCase 2: similar, n6 removable s0. Same outcome.\n\nSo the algorithm seems to match the pattern: only n6 is removable because it is leaf, not required, no retained children, and required symbols still present elsewhere.\n\nBut what about nodes like n4? They are not removable because symbol is required. n2 not removable because it has retained child n4.\n\nSo output is consistent.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the output format is exactly as required. The instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So the return dict should have keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". I'll use those.\n\nNow write the Python code.\n\nWe'll need to import defaultdict.\n\nEdge: If no nodes are removed, removal_order empty list.\n\nNow produce the final JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a JSON string.\n\nWe must escape newlines, backslashes, and quotes.\n\nI'll write the code as a multi-line string and then use json.dumps to escape it. But since the entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\", we need to output that JSON.\n\nI'll produce the implementation string.\n\nImplementation code:\n\n```\nimport json\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate payload\n    if 'payload' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing payload\")\n    payload = case['payload']\n    if 'nodes' not in payload or 'required_symbols' not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing nodes or required_symbols\")\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\n    # build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children = defaultdict(list)\n    symbol_nodes = defaultdict(set)\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(nid)\n        symbol_nodes[node['symbol']].add(nid)\n    # check root\n    root_id = None\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\n        if node.get('parent') is None:\n            if root_id is not None:\n                raise ValueError(\"Multiple roots\")\n            root_id = nid\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root\")\n    # ensure required symbols present\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if not symbol_nodes.get(sym):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not found\")\n    # initialize retained set and symbol retained sets (only for required symbols)\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    # we will maintain symbol counts for required symbols\n    req_symbol_retained = {sym: set(symbol_nodes[sym]) for sym in required_symbols}\n    removal_order = []\n    # sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # check required symbols still represented after removal\n        # (only the current node might affect if its symbol is required, but we already skipped)\n        # We still check to be safe: for each required symbol, ensure there is at least one node retained (not counting this node if it had that symbol)\n        # Since node's symbol is not required, we simply verify each required symbol has at least one node in current retained.\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            # check if there is at least one retained node with this symbol\n            if not any(rid in retained for rid in req_symbol_retained[sym]):\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # remove this node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        # update symbol sets for required symbols (optional but not needed for further checks because we already removed)\n        # Actually we need to update for future checks? Since we are only checking for other nodes, but this node's removal might affect other nodes' required symbol checks? But we only check required symbols upon removal of a node, and we already ensured that after removal each required symbol still exists. However, for future nodes, the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" will be checked again, and we want the count to reflect removals. So we need to update the sets.\n        # Actually our check above used any() on retained set, but we didn't update req_symbol_retained